.\" Man page for hallpass-cli.
.\"
.\" Hand-maintained alongside crates/hallpass-cli/src/args.rs, which parses
.\" arguments by hand and is the authority on every command and flag below.
.TH HALLPASS\-CLI 1 2026\-07\-30 "hallpass 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hallpass\-cli \- client for the hallpass application firewall
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hallpass\-cli
.RI [ "GLOBAL OPTIONS" ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hallpass\-cli
is the terminal client for
.BR hallpassd ,
the hallpass daemon. It connects to the daemon's Unix socket, and from there
inspects daemon state, manages rules, follows connection activity, answers
connection prompts, and asks what policy would do with a connection that has
not happened.
.PP
The client only reports and asks; every decision is the daemon's. Talking to
the socket requires membership of the
.B hallpass
group (or root).
.PP
Global options are accepted anywhere on the command line, before or after
.IR COMMAND ,
and are stripped before the command's own options are parsed.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B status
Show daemon statistics, including whether the daemon is enforcing policy or
only observing it.
.TP
.BR rules " [" \-\-stats ]
List the rules the daemon has loaded.
.B \-\-stats
adds a per-rule hit count, which the daemon accounts for at runtime and which
is not part of a rule.
.TP
.BR "rules add " [ OPTIONS ]
Add a rule. See
.B RULES ADD OPTIONS
below;
.B \-\-name
and
.B \-\-action
are required, and every other option narrows what the rule matches.
.TP
.BI "rules rm " NAME
Delete the rule called
.IR NAME .
.TP
.BI "rules toggle " "NAME " on | off
Enable or disable the rule called
.IR NAME .
A disabled rule stays loaded but is skipped when policy is evaluated.
.TP
.B rules export
Write the whole ruleset to standard output as one TOML document. This output
is always TOML, never JSON, because it exists to be saved, diffed and fed back
to
.BR "rules import" ;
.B \-\-json
covers the "pipe it into a program" case through
.BR rules .
.TP
.BI "rules import " PATH
Add every rule in such a document, reporting each one as it goes. A rule the
daemon rejects does not stop the import, so one run reports every problem
rather than only the first, but the command exits non-zero if any rule failed.
.TP
.BR events " [" OPTIONS ]
Stream connection events until interrupted with Ctrl\-C, optionally replaying
recent history first. See
.B EVENTS OPTIONS
below.
.TP
.BR top " [" OPTIONS ]
Live aggregate view of connection activity, redrawn periodically. See
.B TOP OPTIONS
below.
.TP
.B watch
Interactively answer connection prompts as the daemon raises them. This is the
terminal equivalent of the desktop prompt window.
.TP
.BR explain " [" OPTIONS ]
Say what policy would do with a hypothetical connection, and which rule
decides it. Nothing here is checked against the running system: the connection
is described, not observed, so
.B \-\-exe
is a claim rather than a lookup. See
.B EXPLAIN OPTIONS
below.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS EVENTS OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-last " N"
Replay the last
.I N
decided connections before streaming. Clamped to 10000.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-follow
With
.BR \-\-last ,
print the replay and exit instead of streaming. Requires
.BR \-\-last .
.TP
.BI \-\-exe " SUBSTR"
Only events whose executable path contains
.IR SUBSTR .
Repeatable; any of them matching is enough.
.TP
.BI \-\-domain " SUBSTR"
Only events whose destination domain contains
.IR SUBSTR .
Repeatable; any of them matching is enough.
.TP
.BI \-\-verdict " allow" | deny | reject | blocked
Only these verdicts. Repeatable, and
.B blocked
is shorthand for deny or reject. The policy verdict is matched, so a
"would\-deny" event from observe mode is still kept by
.BR "\-\-verdict deny" .
.PP
Filters of the same kind are OR\-ed together; filters of different kinds are
AND\-ed. They are matched against the raw metadata rather than its sanitized
display form, so a hostile executable name is still findable by the very
filter written to hunt for it.
.SS TOP OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-group\-by " exe" | domain | host | port | rule
What each row counts. Default:
.BR exe .
.B domain
falls back to the destination IP when no domain is known.
.TP
.BI \-\-interval " SECS"
Redraw period in seconds. Default: 2. Values below 1 are raised to 1.
.TP
.BI \-\-top " N"
Rows to show. Default: 20, maximum 1000.
.SS EXPLAIN OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-dest " IP"
Destination IP address. Required. A bare address, not a CIDR block: this
describes one connection, and a block has no single address to send to.
.TP
.BI \-\-port " PORT"
Destination port. Required.
.TP
.BI \-\-proto " tcp" | udp
Transport protocol. Default:
.BR tcp .
.TP
.BI \-\-exe " PATH"
Executable path of the hypothetical process.
.TP
.BI \-\-cmdline " STR"
Its full command line.
.TP
.BI \-\-parent\-exe " PATH"
Executable path of its parent.
.TP
.BI \-\-exe\-sha256 " HEX"
SHA\-256 to use for hash operands, as 64 hex digits. Without it the daemon
hashes whatever
.B \-\-exe
names.
.TP
.BI \-\-domain " NAME"
Destination domain, as DNS snooping would have annotated it.
.TP
.BI \-\-user " UID"
UID of the hypothetical process.
.TP
.BI \-\-src " IP"
Source IP address. Defaults to the unspecified address of the destination's
own family, so an IPv6 destination is not reported as failing to match for a
reason nobody asked about.
.TP
.BI \-\-src\-port " PORT"
Source port. Default: 0.
.TP
.BI \-\-iface " NAME"
Outbound network interface, for example
.BR eth0 .
.TP
.BI \-\-app\-id " ID"
Packaged application the hypothetical process belongs to, as
.BR flatpak: <app\-id>
or
.BR snap: <name>.
.SS RULES ADD OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-name " NAME"
Rule name. Required.
.TP
.BI \-\-action " allow" | deny | reject
Action on match. Required.
.B deny
drops the packet;
.B reject
answers so the connection fails at once.
.TP
.BI \-\-exe " PATH"
Exact executable path.
.TP
.BI \-\-exe\-glob " GLOB"
Glob matched against the executable path.
.TP
.BI \-\-exe\-sha256 " HEX"
SHA\-256 of the executable, as 64 hex digits.
.TP
.BI \-\-dest " IP" | CIDR
Destination IP address or CIDR block.
.TP
.BI \-\-port " PORT"
Destination port.
.TP
.BI \-\-domain " DOMAIN"
Domain, either exact or of the form
.BR *.suffix .
.TP
.BI \-\-user " UID"
UID of the initiating process.
.TP
.BI \-\-proto " tcp" | udp
Transport protocol.
.TP
.BI \-\-cmdline\-contains " STR"
Substring of the process command line.
.TP
.BI \-\-parent\-exe " PATH"
Exact executable path of the parent process.
.TP
.BI \-\-src " IP" | CIDR
Source IP address or CIDR block.
.TP
.BI \-\-src\-port " PORT"
Source port.
.TP
.BI \-\-iface " NAME"
Outbound network interface, for example
.BR eth0 .
.TP
.BI \-\-app\-id " ID"
Packaged application, as
.BR flatpak: <app\-id>
or
.BR snap: <name>,
read from the process's cgroup and matched exactly. A cgroup name is chosen
by whoever created it, so this scopes a rule rather than enforcing it.
.TP
.BI \-\-domains\-file " PATH"
File of domains, in hosts format or one per line, matched against the
destination domain.
.TP
.BI \-\-ips\-file " PATH"
File of destination IP addresses or CIDR blocks, one per line.
.TP
.BI \-\-hashes\-file " PATH"
File of executable SHA\-256 hashes, one per line.
.IP
These three paths are read by the daemon, not by the client, and the daemon
accepts only files sitting directly in its rules directory
.RI ( /etc/hallpass/rules.d/
by default). A path anywhere else is rejected.
.TP
.BI \-\-duration " session" | forever | TIMESPAN
Rule lifetime. Default:
.BR forever .
.B session
drops the rule when the daemon restarts, and a
.I TIMESPAN
such as
.BR 30s ", " 5m ", " 2h " or " 1d
expires it after that long. Only
.B forever
rules are written to the rules directory; the other two live in the running
daemon and nowhere else.
.TP
.BI \-\-priority " N"
Priority. Higher wins. Default: 0.
.SS GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-socket " PATH"
Daemon socket. Default:
.IR /run/hallpass/hallpass.sock .
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit machine-readable JSON for
.BR status ", " rules ", " events
(one object per line),
.B top
and
.BR explain .
It also turns color off, since colored JSON is nobody's idea of machine
readable.
.TP
.BI \-\-color " auto" | always | never
Colorize output. Default:
.BR auto ,
meaning only when standard output is a terminal and
.B NO_COLOR
is unset.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show usage and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
A usage error, an unreadable or unparsable file, or an error reported by the
daemon. A
.B rules import
in which any rule was rejected also exits 1.
.TP
.B 2
The daemon could not be reached: the socket is missing, unreachable, or the
connection was lost mid-conversation.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/hallpass/config.toml
Daemon configuration. Read by
.BR hallpassd ,
not by this client.
.TP
.I /etc/hallpass/rules.d/
Rule files loaded by the daemon, where
.B rules add
writes a
.B \-\-duration forever
rule, and the only directory the match-list options may name a file in.
.TP
.I /run/hallpass/hallpass.sock
The daemon socket this client connects to. Override it with
.BR \-\-socket .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
If set to a non-empty value, output is not colorized under
.BR "\-\-color auto" .
.B "\-\-color always"
overrides it.
.SH EXAMPLES
Allow one program to reach one domain, permanently:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
hallpass\-cli rules add \-\-name curl\-example \-\-action allow \e
    \-\-exe /usr/bin/curl \-\-domain example.org \-\-port 443
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show what was blocked recently, without waiting for more:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
hallpass\-cli events \-\-last 200 \-\-no\-follow \-\-verdict blocked
.EE
.RE
.PP
Ask why a connection would be refused, and which rule decides it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
hallpass\-cli explain \-\-exe /usr/bin/ssh \-\-dest 192.0.2.10 \-\-port 22
.EE
.RE
.PP
Save the ruleset, then restore it on another machine:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
hallpass\-cli rules export > rules.toml
hallpass\-cli rules import rules.toml
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR nft (8),
.BR nftables (8)
.PP
The README in the hallpass source tree covers installation, the daemon
configuration file and the rule file format.
